Let $Z$ be the vertex opposite the hypotenuse, and let $X$ be the vertex opposite the side of length $12 \sqrt{2}.$

We want to find the length of $XY.$ Let $Z'$ be the point on $ZY$ such that $XZ' = XZ.$

[asy]
unitsize(1inch);
pair P,Q,R,Z,Z1;
P = (0,0);
Q= (1,0);
R = (0,1);
draw (P--Q--R--P,linewidth(0.9));
draw(rightanglemark(Q,P,R,3));
label("$X$",P,S);
label("$Y$",Q,S);
label("$Z$",R,N);
label("$12\sqrt{2}$",(Q+R)/2,NE);
label("$45^\circ$",(0.7,0),N);
draw(P--(0.5,0));
draw((0.5,0)--(0.5,0.5),dashed);
label("$Z'$",(0.5,0.5),N);
[/asy]

Now, we can use the Pythagorean Theorem in $\triangle XZ'Z$ to find $XZ'.$
\begin{align*}
XZ'^2 + XZ'^2 &= Z'Z^2 \\
2XZ'^2 &= 12^2 \\
XZ'^2 &= 72 \\
XZ' &= \sqrt{72} = 6 \sqrt{2}.
\end{align*}

Now, we can use the Pythagorean Theorem in $\triangle XYZ'$ to find $XY.$
\begin{align*}
XZ'^2 + YZ'^2 &= XY^2 \\
(6 \sqrt{2})^2 + YZ'^2 &= XY^2 \\
72 + YZ'^2 &= XY^2 \\
72 + (12 \sqrt{2})^2 &= XY^2 \\
72 + 288 &= XY^2 \\
360 &= XY^2 \\
XY &= \sqrt{360} = \boxed{6 \sqrt{10}}.
\end{align*}. The answer is 6\sqrt{10}
